Medical ethics is an important field that we need to master.
If we can't, for example, come up with good policies regarding
assisted suicide suspensions, and good ethics arguments acceptable
to the local doctors and coroners to back them up, then we will
be considered unethical, and non-cryonicists will come up with 
those policies for us.

Ray Cromwell:
>It just makes me irritated to even see medical ethicists speak. It seems
>like a bogus field to be in. I can just imagine a future cryonics
>court case where one of them is called in as an "expert witness"

One can just as easily, and more optimistically, imagine medical ethicists 
making expert testimonies in our favor, if we take up the challenge of 
learning the field.  By using widely accepted axioms to argue for 
our form of medical ethics, we can provide convincing arguments for 
our expert witnesses.

