someone on the list wrote:

>Another area in which Extropians are too dogmatic for my taste is in
>cryonics.  It is continually implied that anyone who isn't signed up
>for cryonics is "deathist" and un-extropian.  I am not signed up, and
>don't plan to.  Why?  Because what is most important to me is the

I too disagree with the emphasis on cryonics but for exactly the opposite
reason. Namely I think the emphasis on cryonics takes attention away from
the real opportunity which is physical immortality - the direct approach,
(by not dying). Cryonics seems to postpone the problem at best, while
introducing many new problems. So let's solve the eventual problem first
and skip the others.

The biggest problems I encounter in talking with others about immortality
are psychological, not technical or practical. I run into a psychological
resistance in others which seems natural when you consider how many basic
life choices must be reconsidered from the point of view of planning to
live forever. 

Making the psychological leap to planning for immortality is much the same
as living each day as if it was my last. Either way there is no escaping
the results of my actions today. Just making the psychological adjustment
has immediate benefits, though after a while the incongruence between
planning to live forever and not doing anything about it would probably
become a drag.

So what are the technical challenges? Here is what I see:

1. Extend lifespan to 100+ years through better external conditions (diet,
caloric restriction, less pollutants, safety, avoiding certain diseases)

2. Uncover the major mechanisms of aging (ie. biological clock) develop
strategies to fool cells to reproduce as if the body was younger.

3. Uncover the secondary mechanisms of aging (Hayflick limit, etc). Develop
counter strategies.

4. Tinker with alternate bodies (AI's) and methods to transport
consciousness or selfhood (uploading).

Timetable:  I'm 39 years old so if this is going to work for me I'll need
#1 in 50 years or less. Suppose #2 and #3 combined can double human
lifespan (I think this is conservative). Then I'll need #4 by mid- 22nd
century. Considering that 50 years ago geneticists hadn't discovered DNA
this seems like a a workable timetable to me. 

Whaddaya think?


