From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Mon, 21 Nov 1994 14:48:39 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-11-300] immortality

James Baker: 
> 1. Extend lifespan to 100+ years through better external conditions (diet,
> caloric restriction, less pollutants, safety, avoiding certain diseases)

None of these have been demonstrated to raise life expectency to above
100, or even above 90, in humans.  Millions of people have been working
very hard in all these areas for decades with only a few years worth
of improvement on adult life expectency.  The two main areas of
life expectency improvement in this century have come from better sanitation
and preventiion of childhood disease.  The life expectency of adults
has not recieved a major boost from medicine or personal prevention,
and is not likely to do so over the next few decades.  

> 2. Uncover the major mechanisms of aging (ie. biological clock) develop
> strategies to fool cells to reproduce as if the body was younger.

It's almost completely unknown whether this can be accomplished, or how.

> 3. Uncover the secondary mechanisms of aging (Hayflick limit, etc). Develop
> counter strategies.

Not clear whether this will buy us much.   Rather, Hayflick et. al.'s
work indicates that there is a fine line between cellular life extension
and cancer (which is what you get when you turn off the cellular
biological clock).

> 4. Tinker with alternate bodies (AI's) and methods to transport
> consciousness or selfhood (uploading).

There are still major philosophical and quantum physics questions
about whether emulating consciousness on a Turing machine is
even possible.   Even less is it known whether crudely emulating
a brain's neurons, which we will be able to do in c. 30 years at current
rates of hardware, or detailed emulation of a brains's synaptic and 
hormonal chemical interactions, which will take much longer, will be 
sufficient to emulate consciousness.   The far-future possibility
uploading is certainly something to take into account in deciding
how important other life extension techiques to get that far are,
but uploading as a near-term alternative to cryonics and life
extension is silly.

> Timetable:  I'm 39 years old so if this is going to work for me I'll need
> #1 in 50 years or less. 

#1 has not had a major impact on life expectency in adults, and is
unlikely to over the next 50 years.  It's a good idea to hedge your 
bets. (2-4) are even less probable within the next few decades, although 
promising in the long run, thus something to take into account in deciding
what steps one can take today to improve the odds of making it
to the long run.

