Date: Mon, 21 Nov 1994 17:53:31 -0600
Subject: [#94-11-302] Wavelets, Image compression

I think there are some misconceptions going around here about
image compression formats.

MPEG is a standard, but the difference between mpeg1 and mpeg2,
as far as I could tell from reading the compression faq's, isn't
in the algorithm itself but in the volume and quality of the
data being compressed. MPEG2 is, AFAIK, the same as MPEG1. Changing
from one to the other isn't going to help image quality much.

I suspect that there may be much difference in algorithm implementation
in many of the various MPEG's out there. It may simply be the real-time
nature of the compression that is causing Tim May's headaches.

Disclaimer: it's very possible that I'm wrong about all this.

Phil

